# Exercise 02: System Information and Network Commands

**Author:** kuronoi  
**Project:** Windows System Administration Lab

## 1. Objective
To understand Windows Command Prompt commands used to retrieve computer information and inspect network configuration.

## 2. Scenario
An IT support technician needs to identify a computer, inspect its network settings and test connectivity to another network device.

This was a simulated training exercise.

## 3. Commands Learned

| Command | Function |
|---|---|
| `ipconfig` | Displays basic IP configuration |
| `ipconfig /all` | Displays detailed network configuration |
| `hostname` | Displays the computer name |
| `ping` | Tests network connectivity |
| `systeminfo` | Displays detailed system information |

## 4. Practical Procedure
1. Open Command Prompt.
2. Use `hostname` to identify the computer name.
3. Use `ipconfig` to inspect the IP address, subnet mask and default gateway.
4. Use `ipconfig /all` to inspect detailed network information, including DNS servers and MAC address.
5. Use `ping` to test connectivity to an authorised network destination.
6. Use `systeminfo` to retrieve detailed Windows system information.

## 5. Results
The exercise demonstrated how Command Prompt can be used to inspect computer details, retrieve network configuration and perform basic connectivity checks.

The results are based on simulated training scenarios. No actual command execution results are claimed.

## 6. Lessons Learned
- `ipconfig` displays basic IP configuration.
- `ipconfig /all` provides detailed network information.
- `hostname` identifies the computer name.
- `ping` helps test network connectivity.
- `systeminfo` provides detailed operating system and hardware information.

## 7. Conclusion
This exercise improved my understanding of Windows system information and network diagnostic commands. These commands are useful in IT support and basic network troubleshooting.

